{"id":"1f9e5fae206b6d73b37518565943f7524921267d","axis":"silent","repo":"nrwl/nx","sha":"1f9e5fae206b6d73b37518565943f7524921267d","parsed":{"files":[{"path":"astro-docs/src/content/docs/kb/configure-outputs.mdoc","lines":["@@ -39,6 +39,14 @@ Outputs use the same [glob syntax](/docs/reference/inputs#glob-syntax) as inputs"," Outputs can also be determined from the `options` of running a task via the `{options.[propertyName]}` syntax."," This is useful when an option for the task determines the output location and could be modified when the task is run."," This path is resolved from the root of the workspace.","+An output whose option isn't set, or is empty, is left out.","+To cache another location in that case, list fallbacks after `??`.","+Nx uses the first one that is set and not empty, and the last one can be a path.","+For a project at `apps/myapp`, `{options.outputPath ?? {workspaceRoot}/dist/{projectRoot}}`","+caches `dist/apps/myapp` when `outputPath` isn't set.","+Outputs can't read environment variables.","+Nx resolves them once, when it creates the task graph,","+and the task can run on a machine with a different environment."," "," If an output file or directory does not exist, it will be ignored."," ","@@ -57,7 +65,7 @@ View the project's configuration to see a list of the outputs which are defined"," ## Configure outputs"," "," The tasks you run in your workspace will likely already have `outputs` defined.","-Be sure to [view the existing outputs](#viewing-outputs-of-a-task) and start from there.","+Be sure to [view the existing outputs](#view-outputs-of-a-task) and start from there."," "," Nx Plugins often [infer outputs for tasks](/docs/concepts/mental-model#inferred-tasks) which run other tools."," Nx Plugins will look at the configuration files and/or command-line-arguments of the tools in your workspace to understand the outputs of running those tools.","@@ -70,7 +78,8 @@ In some cases, Nx plugins may not infer the outputs of a task as you expect, the"," - The `outputs` array for a specific target in the project configuration file."," "," {% aside title=\"Copy the existing outputs before modifying outputs for a task\" %}","-To override the `outputs` of a task, start by copying over the entire array shown when [viewing the project details](#viewing-the-outputs-of-a-task) and then add/modify/remove outputs as needed.","+To override the `outputs` of a task, start by copying over the entire array shown when viewing the","+project details and then add/modify/remove outputs as needed."," {% /aside %}"," "," As you configure `outputs`, keep the project details screen open and it will refresh as changes are made. Check to make sure that the intended configuration is shown."],"additions":11,"deletions":2,"truncated":false},{"path":"astro-docs/src/content/docs/reference/nx-json.mdoc","lines":["@@ -598,6 +598,10 @@ For more information on how `nx release` works, see [manage releases](/docs/feat"," "," The full list of configuration options available for `\"release\"` can be found here: [https://github.com/nrwl/nx/blob/master/packages/nx/src/config/nx-json.ts](https://github.com/nrwl/nx/blob/master/packages/nx/src/config/nx-json.ts) under `NxReleaseConfiguration`."," ","+Placeholders in release configuration, such as `{version}` in a tag pattern,","+can't list fallbacks with `??`.","+Only Docker `versionSchemes` can read environment variables with `{env.*}`.","+"," ### Projects"," "," If you want to limit the projects that `nx release` targets, you can use the `projects` property in `nx.json` to do so. This property is either a string, or an array of strings. The strings can be project names, glob patterns, directories, tag references or anything else that is supported by the `--projects` filter you may know from other commands such as `nx run`."],"additions":4,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"astro-docs/src/content/docs/reference/project-configuration.mdoc","lines":["@@ -652,11 +652,21 @@ Then set `waitFor` to `ready` on the `dependsOn` entry of each task that should","   A `prefix` set on an `nx:run-commands` command is part of the matched text.","   Color codes and other terminal control sequences are removed before matching."," ","-Strings in `readyWhen` can use the `{workspaceRoot}`, `{projectRoot}`, `{projectName}` and `{options.*}` placeholders.","+Strings in `readyWhen` can use the `{workspaceRoot}`, `{projectRoot}`, `{projectName}`,","+`{options.*}`, and `{env.*}` placeholders."," `{options.*}` reads the options the executor receives: the target options, the configuration in use, the command-line overrides and the executor's defaults."," So `\"url\": \"http://localhost:{options.port}\"` follows `--port`, and works without a `port` option when the executor has a default for it."," `port` accepts such a string too, for example `\"{options.port}\"`.","+`{env.*}` reads the environment the task runs with, including its `.env` files.","+For an `nx:run-commands` target, that includes its `env` and `envFile` options.","+The variable name can contain only the letters `a` to `z` in either case, digits, `_`, `.`, `-`,","+and parentheses.","+A placeholder can list fallbacks after `??`, and Nx uses the first one that is set and not empty.","+The last fallback can be plain text, as in `\"{options.port ?? env.PORT ?? 4200}\"`.","+A placeholder that resolves to no value fails the task before it starts,","+with an error that names the placeholder."," Other text in braces, such as a `{{.State.Health.Status}}` template in a command, is left as it is.","+In `port` and `host`, such text fails the task instead."," "," The optional `timeout` key is how long Nx waits for readiness, in milliseconds."," It defaults to `60000`."],"additions":11,"deletions":1,"truncated":false},{"path":"e2e/nx/src/extras.test.ts","lines":["@@ -448,6 +448,29 @@ NX_USERNAME=$FIRSTNAME $LASTNAME`","       expect(result).toContain('hello1');","       expect(result).toContain('hello2');","     });","+","+    it('should resolve envFile references against the task env when run directly or forked', () => {","+      updateFile('.env', (content) => `${content}\\nNX_E2E_PORT=3000`);","+      updateFile(`libs/${libName}/.env`, 'NX_E2E_PORT=5000');","+      updateFile(","+        `libs/${libName}/.serve.env`,","+        'NX_E2E_URL=http://localhost:${NX_E2E_PORT}'","+      );","+      updateJson(join('libs', libName, 'project.json'), (config) => {","+        config.targets['print-url'] = {","+          command: 'echo \"[$NX_E2E_PORT][$NX_E2E_URL]\"',","+          options: { envFile: `libs/${libName}/.serve.env` },","+        };","+        return config;","+      });","+","+      for (const env of [{}, { NX_RUN_COMMANDS_DIRECTLY: 'false' }]) {","+        const result = runCLI(`run ${libName}:print-url --skip-nx-cache`, {","+          env,","+        });","+        expect(result).toContain('[5000][http://localhost:5000]');","+      }","+    });","   });"," ","   describe('task graph inputs', () => {"],"additions":23,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"packages/nx/docs/run-commands-examples.md","lines":["@@ -90,6 +90,19 @@ or simply with:"," nx run frontend:create-script --name=example"," ```"," ","+An argument that isn't passed resolves to an empty string. To use another value, list fallbacks after `??`. Nx uses the first one that is set and not empty. A fallback can read an environment variable of the command through `env.NAME`, including variables from the `env` and `envFile` options. `NAME` can contain only the letters `a` to `z` in either case, digits, `_`, `.`, `-`, and parentheses. The last fallback can be plain text:","+","+```json","+\"serve-static\": {","+    \"executor\": \"nx:run-commands\",","+    \"options\": {","+        \"command\": \"http-server dist --port={args.port ?? env.PORT ?? 8080}\"","+    }","+}","+```","+","+The resolved command is part of the task's terminal output, which Nx caches when the target is cacheable. To keep a secret out of that output, read it as a shell variable instead, such as `$TOKEN`.","+"," Keep in mind that any command in the list that does not include interpolation will still have all passed arguments forwarded to it by default. For example, given:"," "," ```json"],"additions":13,"deletions":0,"truncated":false},{"path":"packages/nx/schemas/nx-schema.json","lines":["@@ -625,7 +625,7 @@","   },","   \"definitions\": {","     \"readyWhen\": {","-      \"description\": \"How Nx detects that this continuous target is ready. Dependents opt in with \\\"waitFor\\\": \\\"ready\\\" on their dependsOn entry. Strings may use the {workspaceRoot}, {projectRoot}, {projectName} and {options.*} placeholders, resolved against the options the task runs with.\",","+      \"description\": \"How Nx detects that this continuous target is ready. Dependents opt in with \\\"waitFor\\\": \\\"ready\\\" on their dependsOn entry. Strings may use the {workspaceRoot}, {projectRoot}, {projectName}, {options.*}, and {env.*} placeholders, resolved against the options and environment the task runs with. A placeholder can list fallbacks, as in {options.port ?? env.PORT ?? 4200}.\",","       \"type\": \"object\",","       \"properties\": {","         \"url\": {"],"additions":1,"deletions":1,"truncated":false},{"path":"packages/nx/schemas/project-schema.json","lines":["@@ -254,7 +254,7 @@","   },","   \"definitions\": {","     \"readyWhen\": {","-      \"description\": \"How Nx detects that this continuous target is ready. Dependents opt in with \\\"waitFor\\\": \\\"ready\\\" on their dependsOn entry. Strings may use the {workspaceRoot}, {projectRoot}, {projectName} and {options.*} placeholders, resolved against the options the task runs with.\",","+      \"description\": \"How Nx detects that this continuous target is ready. Dependents opt in with \\\"waitFor\\\": \\\"ready\\\" on their dependsOn entry. Strings may use the {workspaceRoot}, {projectRoot}, {projectName}, {options.*}, and {env.*} placeholders, resolved against the options and environment the task runs with. A placeholder can list fallbacks, as in {options.port ?? env.PORT ?? 4200}.\",","       \"type\": \"object\",","       \"properties\": {","         \"url\": {"],"additions":1,"deletions":1,"truncated":false},{"path":"packages/nx/src/command-line/release/changelog.ts","lines":["@@ -12,7 +12,7 @@ import {","   createProjectFileMapUsingProjectGraph,"," } from '../../project-graph/file-map-utils';"," import { createProjectGraphAsync } from '../../project-graph/project-graph';","-import { interpolate } from '../../tasks-runner/utils';","+import { interpolateReleaseConfig } from './utils/interpolate-release-config';"," import { handleErrors } from '../../utils/handle-errors';"," import { isCI } from '../../utils/is-ci';"," import { output } from '../../utils/output';","@@ -40,6 +40,7 @@ import {","   ResolvedCreateRemoteReleaseProvider,","   createNxReleaseConfig,","   handleNxReleaseConfigError,","+  validateCommitMessageTokens,"," } from './config/config';"," import { deepMergeJson } from './config/deep-merge-json';"," import { ReleaseGroupWithName } from './config/filter-release-groups';","@@ -150,6 +151,9 @@ export function createAPI(","     if (configError) {","       return await handleNxReleaseConfigError(configError);","     }","+    if (args.gitCommitMessage) {","+      validateCommitMessageTokens(args.gitCommitMessage);","+    }","     // --print-config exits directly as it is not designed to be combined with any other programmatic operations","     if (args.printConfig) {","       return printConfigAndExit({","@@ -987,7 +991,7 @@ async function generateChangelogForWorkspace({"," ","   let interpolatedTreePath = config.file || '';","   if (interpolatedTreePath) {","-    interpolatedTreePath = interpolate(interpolatedTreePath, {","+    interpolatedTreePath = interpolateReleaseConfig(interpolatedTreePath, {","       projectName: '', // n/a for the workspace changelog","       projectRoot: '', // n/a for the workspace ch